<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">1954 Guatemalan coup d'état</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Operation PBSuccess</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 1954 Guatemalan coup d'état overthrew the elected president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobo-arbenz">Jacobo Árbenz</a>, whose land reform had expropriated unused land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit Company</a>. Organised by the CIA under the code name Operation PBSuccess, it combined an invasion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> by a small force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-castillo-armas">Carlos Castillo Armas</a> with propaganda and air raids; the army refused to fight, and Árbenz resigned on 27 June 1954. Decades of military rule followed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/1954-guatemalan-coup-detat.jpg" alt="1954 Guatemalan coup d'état" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Eisenhower_and_John_Foster_Dulles_in_1956.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Guatemalan Revolution began in 1944, when a popular uprising led by students and middle-class citizens toppled the military ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-ubico">Jorge Ubico</a>. Juan José Arévalo, elected president, introduced a minimum wage, a liberal labour code and wider suffrage. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobo-arbenz">Jacobo Árbenz</a>, elected in 1950, drafted an agrarian reform bill that transferred uncultivated land from large landowners to poor laborers. Árbenz also legalised the communist Guatemalan Party of Labour, though he chose a moderate capitalist approach. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> government, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, was predisposed to see the revolution as communist.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-fruit-company">United Fruit Company</a>, the largest landowner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, had 550,000 acres of which only 15 percent was cultivated, so most of it came under the agrarian law of 1952. It lobbied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to act. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-s-truman">Harry S. Truman</a> authorised Operation PBFortune in 1952, which was aborted quickly. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a>, who had promised to roll back <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, authorised the CIA to carry out Operation PBSuccess in August 1953.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The operation had a budget of 2.7 million dollars for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psychological-warfare">psychological warfare</a> and political action, and the CIA armed and trained a force of 480 men led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-castillo-armas">Carlos Castillo Armas</a>. Its plans included lists of people to be assassinated. Before the invasion, propaganda reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> and a campaign of threats was directed at likely loyalists of Árbenz. Castillo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arma-people">Armas</a> crossed the border on 18 June 1954, while aircraft bombed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> and Chiquimula.</p><p>The invasion force did poorly in the field, and most of its offensives were defeated. But the bombing and fear of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> invasion unsettled the Guatemalan army. Árbenz at first meant to arm civilians but gave arms only to the army. By 25 June officers at Zacapa were reporting that the army would not fight, and they asked him to resign. He did so on 27 June. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> followed, and Castillo Armas became president ten days later after negotiations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Castillo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arma-people">Armas</a> banned opposition parties, imprisoned and tortured opponents and reversed the revolution's social reforms. In the first months his government executed many supporters of Árbenz, with estimates from hundreds to five thousand. He was assassinated by a member of his guard in 1957. The coup was widely criticised abroad and strengthened anti-American sentiment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. The CIA's Operation PBHistory sought evidence of Soviet influence among Árbenz-era documents and found none. A civil war began in 1960 and lasted until 1996. In 1999 President Bill Clinton apologised for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> support of military regimes, and in 2011 President Álvaro Colom publicly apologised to Árbenz's family.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “1954 Guatemalan coup d'état” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A young Argentine doctor, Ernesto Guevara, was living in Guatemala City when the <b>CIA-backed coup</b> toppled Árbenz in 1954, an experience that radicalised the future ‘Che’.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
